Under NC Medicaid, the **general eligibility criteria for CAP/DA services** are that the person must first be a **current NC Medicaid beneficiary**, and then must meet both the policy’s **general coverage standards** and the **CAP/DA-specific waiver criteria**. The current CAP/DA clinical policy is effective **September 1, 2025**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## 1) Basic Medicaid eligibility
A beneficiary must be **enrolled in NC Medicaid**. The policy also notes that a beneficiary’s **eligibility category may carry service restrictions** that can make the person ineligible for CAP/DA services. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 2.1.1

## 2) General coverage standards
CAP/DA services must satisfy NC Medicaid’s general “covered” criteria. In general, the service must be:

- **individualized, specific, and consistent** with the person’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and **not more than the person needs**;
- able to be **safely furnished**, with **no equally effective and more conservative or less costly treatment available statewide**; and
- provided in a way **not primarily for the convenience** of the beneficiary, the caregiver, or the provider. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.1

## 3) CAP/DA-specific eligibility criteria
Medicaid covers CAP/DA when the beneficiary also meets these specific criteria:

- **Meets the required HCBS nursing facility level of care (LOC)** as determined by NC Medicaid, and needs long-term care support at a level typically provided in an institution, related to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need, **assessed quarterly**;
- a completed **CAP assessment** shows a **reasonable indication** the person will need at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within **30 calendar days** of the evaluation;
- has a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services to be provided monthly, to help prevent institutionalization through coordinated case management;
- or has a signed service plan authorizing short-term **equipment, modification, or technology** needed to return to or remain in the primary private residence and prevent institutional placement, with installation completed within **three calendar months** of service plan approval;
- is **able to maintain health, safety, and well-being** at the primary private residence or another approved location using both **formal and informal supports**;
- the person’s **paid care needs can be maintained within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- has a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- has an **emergency back-up and disaster plan** identifying who will provide care when key staff or a primary caregiver cannot do so, including during public health emergencies. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.2.1

## 4) Age/scope point
The policy materials surfaced for this question indicate CAP/DA case-management entity requirements include experience serving **adults age 18 and older**, which is consistent with CAP/DA being the program for disabled adults. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 6.4.1

## Bottom line
A person is generally eligible for CAP/DA services if they:

1. are **enrolled in NC Medicaid** and not barred by their eligibility category;
2. need services that meet NC Medicaid’s general coverage standards;
3. meet the **HCBS nursing facility level of care** and other waiver-specific CAP/DA requirements; and
4. can be safely maintained in the community with an approved service plan and available supports.  

